    "result_text": "The change is complete and the build is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec asked to return `membershipTier: 'SILVER'` when `namesakeCount` is 0 and an email is present, otherwise `'BRONZE'`.\n\n`membershipTier` is a derived response field on the owner, exactly like the existing `membershipNumber`/`householdId`/`namesakeCount` fields. I followed that established pattern with two small, cohesive edits and no new class:\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the `membershipTier` read-only property to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` gains the field.\n\n2. **`src/main/java/.../mapper/OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 added one `@Mapping` expression on `toOwnerDto` that computes the tier:\n   ```java\n   Integer.valueOf(0).equals(owner.getNamesakeCount())\n       && owner.getEmail() != null && !owner.getEmail().isBlank()\n       ? \"SILVER\" : \"BRONZE\"\n   ```\n   The null-safe `Integer.valueOf(0).equals(...)` guards against a missing count without adding branching complexity.\n\n**Structural impact kept low:** two files touched; the only function changed is the bodyless interface method `toOwnerDto`, which gains a single annotation line (CC 1, minimal surrounding complexity). No method or class grows in logic, and no new class multiplies the file count.\n\nTests: `AcceptanceTest` (`coreSilverWhenUniqueWithEmail`, `functionalityBronzeWhenNoEmail`) both pass \u2014 `Tests run: 2, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`, `BUILD SUCCESS`.",
